-- #1044 regression fixture — AN IMPORTED TYPE MUST NOT STEAL AN INTERFACE'S SLOT.
--
-- Read test/references_fixtures/iface_ty_collide/expected.golden as the
-- assertion: `a|method|mfoo` must hold BOTH a.mdk's declaration and the head
-- inside `impl Foo P`, and NO `b|method|mfoo` key may exist at all — module `b`
-- declares no method.
--
-- #1007 fixed a real S0 (impl-method heads keyed with no interface identity) by
-- resolving the impl header's interface name through `nsTy` instead of
-- `nsMethod`. But `nsTy` has the SAME one-slot-per-name property: it is shared by
-- every `data`/`newtype`/`alias`/`interface`, and `importNs` is last-write-wins.
-- So `import b.{Foo}` — an ordinary DATA TYPE — overwrote the slot `import
-- a.{Foo}` had just filled, and the head was filed under a fabricated
-- `b|method|mfoo` key while `a|method|mfoo` lost it. Moving a lookup to a rarer
-- namespace is not the same as making it collision-proof; that is how #1007
-- created #1044.
--
-- The fix keys the lookup in the namespace THE LANGUAGE ITSELF resolves an impl
-- header in — resolve.mdk keeps `expInterfaces` apart from `expTypes` and rejects
-- `impl Foo P` outright ("Unknown interface: Foo") when `Foo` is a data type — so
-- a `data` can no longer enter the slot at all.
--
-- ⚠️ THE IMPORT ORDER BELOW IS LOAD-BEARING. The interface must be imported
-- FIRST and the type SECOND: that is the order in which the type overwrites the
-- interface. Swapping these two lines makes the OLD code produce the RIGHT answer
-- — which is itself the sharpest evidence the old key was wrong, since the result
-- depended on the textual order of two unrelated imports. Do not "tidy" them.
import a.{Foo, mfoo}
import b.{Foo}

public export data P =
  | P

impl Foo P where
  mfoo P = 1

export useIt : Int
useIt = mfoo P
